{"id": "3b0a8201-554e-40da-acd3-2e1d07c35a87", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T00:30:53.274263", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "fc388e73-b7c8-440a-9bfd-d3404d822303", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-2bbf97de-6941-47fa-8ac5-42de1b6a786d", "tool_call": {"id": "call-2bbf97de-6941-47fa-8ac5-42de1b6a786d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Hand off proposed approach for review\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## Plan: Scale × Pretraining × Alignment ablation around Llama-2\\n\\n### 1. Inspection of supplied inputs\\n\\n- `/workspace` contains only `task.json`. The `documents.track.md` field is a one-line pointer (\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\") rather than a real on-disk document. No datasets, code, weights, or environment hints are present.\\n- Environment: Python 3.12, 24 vCPU, 125 GiB RAM, no GPU (`nvidia-smi` not available), no network — consistent with a design-only deliverable. I will not attempt to fetch weights or run models.\\n- Constraint implication: every claim in the design must be defensible from published Llama-2 / Llama-2-Chat technical reports, scaling-law literature, and third-party evaluation papers, not from local execution.\\n\\n### 2. Proposed approach (high level)\\n\\nA 2 × 3 × 2 factorial ablation that crosses **model size**, **pretraining sufficiency**, and **alignment tier**, with downstream evaluation on capability, alignment-sensitive, and safety benchmarks. The headline comparison the question asks about — 70B vs. 13B — is decomposed as:\\n\\n| | Aligned (full RLHF) | Under-aligned (no RLHF) |\\n|--|--|--|\\n| **Fully pretrained (full token budget)** | Llama-2-70B-Chat vs. Llama-2-13B-Chat (and -7B-Chat, -34B-Chat if available) | Llama-2-70B base vs. Llama-2-13B base |\\n| **Insufficiently pretrained (token-truncated or undertrained checkpoint)** | Undertrained-Chat variant (or proxy) | Undertrained base variant |\\n\\n**Candidate model set (`M`):**\\n- Llama-2-7B, Llama-2-13B, Llama-2-70B (base, pretrained on full ~2T tokens).\\n- Llama-2-7B-Chat, Llama-2-13B-Chat, Llama-2-70B-Chat (full SFT + RLHF).\\n- \\\"Insufficient pretraining\\\" baseline: a publicly reported undertrained checkpoint at one model size (e.g., a Llama-2 variant trained on 1%, 10%, or 50% of the published token budget — see Assumption A3) OR an ablation from the scaling-laws literature (e.g., Chinchilla-style suboptimal points) at the 13B/70B scale.\\n- \\\"No refined alignment\\\" baseline: the **base** model (pre-SFT and pre-RLHF). Optionally also an \\\"SFT-only / early-instruct\\\" intermediate checkpoint if released.\\n- Optional but high-value additions if accessible: Llama-2-7B/13B/70B **CodeLlama** variants (different pretraining mix, similar size) as a second `pretraining-tier` data point.\\n\\n**Controlled variables (must be identical across cells):**\\n- Tokenizer (SentencePiece BPE from Llama-2), context length (4096), vocabulary.\\n- Decoding: temperature = 0 (greedy) for capability benchmarks, fixed temperature + 5-seed sampling for variance estimates on generative benchmarks; identical top-p, repetition penalty, max-new-tokens.\\n- Prompt template: the *exact* same ChatML/system prompt for chat variants (the official Llama-2-Chat \\\"[INST] <<SYS>>…\\\" template, no ad-hoc rewording); zero-shot for base, equivalent system message stripped for fair comparison.\\n- Quantization: identical precision across models (bf16 if hardware permits; otherwise int4 with the same GPTQ/AWQ recipe).\\n- Batch composition, in-context exemplars (zero vs. few-shot held fixed per benchmark).\\n- Same evaluation harness (e.g., lm-eval-harness, version pinned), same scorer, same prompt order.\\n\\n**Independent variables (the 3-way factorial):**\\n- IV-1 Scale: {7B, 13B, 70B} (and 34B if available).\\n- IV-2 Pretraining sufficiency: {full budget, truncated budget (or compute-matched smaller)}.\\n- IV-3 Alignment tier: {base, SFT-only if available, full chat (SFT+RLHF)}.\\n\\n**Dependent variables (the metrics that decide the outcome):**\\n- Capability (DVs that respond to *both* scale and pretraining): MMLU (5-shot), ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, TruthfulQA-MC, GSM8K (8-shot, exact-match), HumanEval (pass@1, pass@10 with 5 seeds), BIG-Bench-Hard subset.\\n- Reasoning ceilings: MATH (4-shot), BBH.\\n- Long-context (70B advantage): needle-in-a-haystack, scrolls QA at 4096.\\n- Alignment-sensitive DVs (should respond to RLHF, **not** to scale alone): AlpacaEval-2 / MT-Bench win-rate against a fixed reference, IFEval (instruction-following), AgentBench-lite if feasible.\\n- Safety / instruction-following refusal proxies: BBQ, ToxiGen, Anthropic-harmless subset (or HarmBench); refusal rate on adversarial prompts; TruthfulQA generation split.\\n- Calibration / uncertainty: token-level NLL, ECE on multiple-choice, expected calibration error on GSM8K derived from sampled-trace agreement.\\n- Human-eval subset (where feasible): 200-prompt double-blind A/B on alignment-relevant categories (helpfulness vs. harmlessness tensions), each rated by ≥3 annotators with Krippendorff's α ≥ 0.7.\\n\\n**Evaluation suite:** combine lm-eval-harness (for deterministic capability/hallucination scores), a generation harness (AlpacaEval, IFEval) for alignment DVs, a human-eval subset for tightly alignment-relevant judgments, and a calibration module. All outputs (raw generations + scores) are versioned and stored so the same checkpoint rerun yields identical numbers.\\n\\n**Analysis plan:**\\n1. **Scaling-law ablation.** Fit a piecewise log–log curve of capability vs. parameters at *fixed* pretraining tier. Compare slopes for base-pretrained and chat-pretrained families to check whether the marginal return of scaling is preserved once alignment is added on top.\\n2. **Alignment-vs-scale decomposition.** For each (size, pretrain-tier) cell, Δ_alignment = score_chat − score_base. Test whether 70B-Chat retains the alignment gap of 13B-Chat; if Δ_alignment collapses or inverts at 70B with truncated pretraining, that is direct evidence that \\\"refined alignment\\\" is doing real work.\\n3. **Ceiling/floor effects.** Flag benchmarks where base-70B and chat-70B are statistically indistinguishable from the dataset ceiling (e.g., saturated MMLU), so a non-result there is *not* misinterpreted as alignment failing.\\n4. **Confound checks.**\\n   - Decoding sensitivity: rerun a subset (e.g., GSM8K, MT-Bench) at 3 temperatures to make sure rank orderings survive.\\n   - Prompt-template sensitivity: rerun with the alternative template from the official repo.\\n   - Precision/quantization effects: rerun a 70B cell in bf16 vs. int4 to quantify quantization drift, since int4 can erase small alignment gains.\\n5. **Confound on \\\"insufficient pretraining\\\".** A truncated-token run and a compute-matched smaller model are *not* the same confound (see Assumption A3). I report both contrasts: (a) same params, fewer tokens; (b) same FLOPs, smaller params. If results diverge, the conclusion is qualified.\\n6. **Power / minimum detectable effect.** With n prompts per benchmark and paired comparisons, report the smallest Δ where we can reject the null with α=0.05 and power 0.8. Anything smaller is explicitly not claimed as significant.\\n7. **Pre-registered decision rule.** For the headline question, declare in advance: \\\"70B ≥ 13B is *not* necessary if (i) the simple effect of scale is non-significant *or* negative on ≥2 of {MMLU, GSM8K, HumanEval} after multiple-comparison correction, **and** (ii) the alignment gap at 70B is ≤ alignment gap at 13B on ≥2 of {AlpacaEval, IFEval, safety proxy}.\\\"\\n\\n**Feasibility assessment:**\\n- *Checkpoints*: Llama-2-7B/13B/70B (base + chat) are publicly available. Undertrained / partially trained checkpoints are the weak link — see Uncertainty U1.\\n- *Compute* (rough, outside this sandbox): bf16 inference of Llama-2-70B on the full eval suite ≈ 2×A100-80GB-weeks per pass; int4 GPTQ cuts that ~3–4×. Total program (incl. 5-seed reruns and ablation reruns) = order of 100–200 A100-80GB-days.\\n- *Datasets/evals*: MMLU, GSM8K, HumanEval, BIG-Bench-Hard, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench, IFEval, BBQ, TruthfulQA, HarmBench are all public; we pin versions.\\n- *Human eval*: 200 prompts × 5 cells × 3 raters ≈ 3,000 ratings; realistic with a small annotator pool.\\n- *Sandbox limitation (this environment)*: cannot run any of the above. All numbers in the final design will be cited from published sources or marked as \\\"to be measured\\\".\\n\\n### 3. Consequential assumptions and uncertainties — for your confirmation\\n\\n**Assumptions I'm proposing (treat as defaults unless you override):**\\n- A1. The Llama-2 family (7B/13B/70B, base + chat) is the canonical and only model family used; third-party models kept out to avoid tokenizer / training-mix confounds.\\n- A2. \\\"Refined alignment\\\" = the full Llama-2-Chat recipe (SFT + iterative RLHF with reward model + rejection-sampling), as published. SFT-only models are reported as a *secondary* contrast, not as the primary \\\"no refined alignment\\\" baseline.\\n- A3. \\\"Insufficient pretraining\\\" is operationalized along **two** axes, both reported: (a) same architecture, truncated token budget (e.g., 0.5T of 2T tokens — published scaling-law points or checkpoint where available); (b) compute-matched smaller model trained longer (a Chinchilla-style reallocation where the 13B/70B budget would have been better spent on a smaller longer-trained model).\\n- A4. Comparisons are run at **fixed inference budget measured in FLOPs per generated token**, not fixed wall-clock, so quantization/decoding differences don't masquerade as scale effects.\\n- A5. Evaluation signals most likely to **detect alignment effects at frontier scale** are AlpacaEval-2 win-rate, IFEval, and refusal-harm tradeoff metrics; capability benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K) are not expected to be primary alignment detectors and are reported as sanity checks only.\\n- A6. The headline claim \\\"70B ≥ 13B is not necessary\\\" is a *falsifiable* statistical claim, not a stylistic one — see pre-registered decision rule above.\\n\\n**Uncertainties to resolve before I produce the full design:**\\n- U1. Are partially-trained Llama-2 checkpoints publicly available, or must the \\\"insufficient pretraining\\\" condition be approximated only via Chinchilla-style reallocations or third-party reproductions (e.g., Together's Pythia-style sentence runs)? This determines whether the design is purely observational or partially experimental.\\n- U2. Do we treat **safety** as a *positive* DV (the chat variant should refuse more on HarmBench while staying helpful — i.e., we reward the alignment gap on a help-harm tradeoff curve) or as a separate DV family? Llama-2 reports both, but the framing changes the decision rule.\\n- U3. Is the comparison expected to be **within-budget** (fixed FLOPs at inference) or **at-list-price** (each model served with its recommended precision)? This is where most \\\"is bigger better?\\\" debates collapse, and I want your call.\\n- U4. Do you want me to include Llama-3 as a *reference point only* (to position the Llama-2 finding), or strictly Llama-2?\\n- U5. Quantization: bf16-only or int4 acceptable? Int4 makes 70B feasible on commodity hardware but introduces a known ~1–2 pt alignment-sensitivity confound.\\n- U6. Should the human-eval subset be in scope, or do we restrict to automatic benchmarks only? This changes feasibility substantially.\\n\\nOnce you confirm or adjust A1–A6 and answer U1–U6 (or tell me to proceed with my proposed defaults), I'll produce the full design with the experiment matrix, evaluation harness, scoring protocol, statistical analysis plan, and feasibility table, supported by repository observations and source-cited rationale.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository inspection: `/workspace` contains only `task.json`; the referenced `track.md` field is the inline string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" Grep across `/`, `/lib`, `/usr` finds no project `*.md` outside system ispell/cuda docs. Environment probe (execute_check) confirms Python 3.12, 24 vCPU, 125 GiB RAM, `nvidia-smi` unavailable, `df` shows 690G free on the overlay but network is disabled — consistent with a design-only deliverable. No datasets, checkpoints, or model code are present, so all design choices must be source-cited rather than measured.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"A6. Headline outcome is a pre-registered statistical rule combining capability non-improvement and alignment-gap collapse at 70B.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"A5. Primary alignment detectors: AlpacaEval-2 win-rate, IFEval, refusal-harm tradeoff. MMLU/GSM8K/HumanEval are capability sanity checks only.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"A4. Comparisons at fixed inference FLOPs per generated token, not fixed wall-clock.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"A3. \\\"Insufficient pretraining\\\" operationalized two ways: (a) same-arch token-truncated run, (b) compute-matched smaller model trained longer (Chinchilla reallocation).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"A2. \\\"Refined alignment\\\" = Llama-2-Chat's full recipe (SFT + iterative RLHF); SFT-only is a secondary contrast.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"A1. Llama-2 family only (7B/13B/70B, base and chat); no third-party models.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"U6. Human-eval in scope, or automatic benchmarks only?</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"U5. Quantization: bf16-only or int4 acceptable?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U4. Include Llama-3 as reference point (for positioning) or restrict to Llama-2?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U3. Comparisons at fixed inference FLOPs vs. at-recommended-precision (\\\"list price\\\")?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U2. Is safety a positive component of the alignment DV (help-harm tradeoff curve) or a separate DV family?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U1. Are partially-trained Llama-2 checkpoints publicly available, or must insufficient-pretraining be approximated from Chinchilla-style points or third-party reproductions?</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-7185c1b9-3c64-4bb6-b325-77b97b07f8fb", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
