EM, HumanEval pass@1 / pass@10 (5 seeds), BIG-Bench-Hard subset.\\n- Reasoning ceilings: MATH 4-shot, BBH.\\n- Long-context (the 70B advantage lever): needle-in-a-haystack, scrolls QA at 4096.\\n- Alignment-sensitive (should respond to RLHF, not scale alone): AlpacaEval-2 / MT-Bench win-rate against a fixed reference, IFEval, AgentBench-lite if feasible.\\n- Safety / refusal proxies: BBQ, ToxiGen, HarmBench; refusal rate on adversarial prompts; TruthfulQA-generation split.\\n- Calibration / hallucination proxies: token-level NLL, ECE on MC, expected calibration error on GSM8K sampled-trace agreement.\\n- Human-eval subset: 200-prompt double-blind A/B on alignment-relevant categories, at least 3 annotators each, Krippendorff alpha at least 0.7.\\n\\nEvaluation suite: lm-eval-harness (deterministic capability/hallucination), a generation harness (AlpacaEval, IFEval) for alignment DVs, a human-eval subset for tightly alignment-relevant judgments, and a calibration module. All raw generations and scores are versioned so reruns are reproducible.\\n\\nAnalysis plan:\\n1. Scaling-law ablation. Fit a piecewise log-log curve of capability vs. parameters at fixed pretraining tier. Compare slopes for base and chat families to check whether the marginal return of scaling is preserved after alignment is layered on.\\n2. Alignment-vs-scale decomposition. For each (size, pretrain-tier) cell, compute Delta_alignment = score_chat - score_base. Test whether 70B-Chat retains the alignment gap of 13B-Chat; if Delta_alignment collapses or inverts at 70B with truncated pretraining, this directly attributes the outcome to refined alignment doing real work.\\n3. Ceiling/floor effects. Flag benchmarks where base-70B and chat-70B are statistically indistinguishable from a dataset ceiling (e.g., saturated MMLU); a non-result there is not misread as alignment failing.\\n4. Confound checks. (a) Decoding sensitivity: rerun GSM8K and MT-Bench at 3 temperatures; (b) prompt-template sensitivity: rerun with the alternative official template; (c) precision sensitivity: rerun a 70B cell in bf16 vs. int4 to quantify quantization drift that can erase small alignment gains.\\n5. Confound on insufficient pretraining itself. A truncated-token run and a compute-matched smaller model are not the same confound (Assumption A3). Report both contrasts: same params but fewer tokens; same FLOPs but smaller params. If they diverge, the conclusion is qualified accordingly.\\n6. Power and minimum detectable effect. With n prompts per benchmark and paired comparisons, report the smallest Delta rejected at alpha=0.05 with power=0.8; smaller effects are not claimed as significant.\\n7. Pre-registered decision rule for the headline question: 70B >= 13B is \\\"not necessary\\\" if (i) the simple effect of scale is non-significant or negative on at least 2 of {MMLU, GSM8K, HumanEval} after multiple-comparison correction, AND (ii) the alignment gap at 70B is at most the alignment gap at 13B on at least 2 of {AlpacaEval, IFEval, safety proxy}.\\n\\nFeasibility assessment:\\n- Checkpoints: Llama-2 7B/13B/70B (base and chat) are public. Undertrained/partially-trained checkpoints are the weak link \\u2014 see Uncertainty U1.\\n- Compute (outside this sandbox): bf16 70B inference on the full eval suite is on the order of 2 A100-80GB-weeks per pass; int4 GPTQ cuts that roughly 3-4x. Total program including 5-seed reruns and ablations is on the order of 100-200 A100-80GB-days.\\n- Datasets/evals: MMLU, GSM8K, HumanEval, BBH, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench, IFEval, BBQ, TruthfulQA, HarmBench \\u2014 all public, version-pinned.\\n- Human eval: 200 prompts x 5 cells x 3 raters is roughly 3,000 ratings; realistic with a small annotator pool.\\n- Sandbox limitation (this environment): cannot run any of the above. Numbers in the final design will be cited from published sources or marked \\\"to be measured\\\".\\n\\n### 3. Consequential assumptions and uncertainties \\u2014 for your confirmation\\n\\nAssumptions I am proposing (defaults unless you override):\\n- A1. Llama-2 family only (7B/13B/70B, base and chat); no third-party models.\\n- A2. \\\"Refined alignment\\\" = Llama-2-Chat's full recipe (SFT + iterative RLHF + rejection sampling). SFT-only models are a secondary contrast, not the primary \\\"no refined alignment\\\" baseline.\\n- A3. \\\"Insufficient pretraining\\\" operationalized along two axes, both reported: (a) same architecture truncated token budget (e.g., 0.5T of 2T tokens \\u2014 published scaling-law points or released checkpoints where available); (b) compute-matched smaller model trained longer (Chinchilla-style reallocation where the 70B/13B budget would have been better spent on a smaller longer-trained model).\\n- A4. Comparisons at fixed inference FLOPs per generated token, not fixed wall-clock, so quantization/decoding differences do not masquerade as scale effects.\\n- A5. Primary alignment detectors at frontier scale: AlpacaEval-2 win-rate, IFEval, refusal-harm tradeoff metrics. MMLU/GSM8K/HumanEval are capability sanity checks only.\\n- A6. Headline claim \\\"70B >= 13B is not necessary\\\" is a pre-registered statistical rule combining capability non-improvement and alignment-gap collapse at 70B.\\n\\nUncertainties to resolve before I produce the full design:\\n- U1. Are partially-trained Llama-2 checkpoints publicly available, or must insufficient-pretraining be approximated only via Chinchilla-style reallocations or third-party reproductions? This determines whether the design is purely observational or partially experimental.\\n- U2. Is safety a positive component of the alignment DV (i.e., reward a chat variant's increase in refusal-while-helpful on a help-harm tradeoff curve), or a separate DV family? Llama-2 reports both; the framing changes the decision rule.\\n- U3. Are comparisons at fixed inference FLOPs (Assumption A4) or at \\\"list-price\\\" precision (each model served at its recommended precision)? This is where most \\\"is bigger better?\\\" debates collapse.\\n- U4. Include Llama-3 as a reference point for positioning, or restrict strictly to Llama-2?\\n- U5. Quantization: bf16-only or int4 acceptable? Int4 makes 70B feasible on commodity hardware but introduces a roughly 1-2 point alignment-sensitivity confound.\\n- U6. Should the human-eval subset be in scope, or restrict to automatic benchmarks only?\\n\\nOnce you confirm or adjust A1-A6 and answer U1-U6 (or tell me to proceed with my proposed defaults), I will produce the full design: complete experiment matrix, evaluation harness, scoring protocol, statistical analysis plan, and feasibility table, supported by source-cited rationale and the repository observations above.\", \"evidence\": \"Repository inspection: /workspace contains only task.json; the referenced track.md field is the inline string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" Grep across /, /lib, /usr finds no project *.md files outside system ispell/cuda docs. Environment probe (execute_check) confirms Python 3.12, 24 vCPU, 125 GiB RAM, nvidia-smi unavailable, df shows 690G free on the overlay, network disabled \\u2014 consistent with a design-only deliverable. No datasets, checkpoints, or model code are present, so all design choices must be source-cited rather than measured.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1. Llama-2 family only (7B/13B/70B base and chat); no third-party models.</item>\\n<item>A2. \\\"Refined alignment\\\" = Llama-2-Chat's full recipe (SFT + iterative RLHF + rejection sampling); SFT-only is a secondary contrast.</item>\\n<item>A3. \\\"Insufficient pretraining\\\" operationalized two ways: (a) same-arch token-truncated run, (b) compute-matched smaller model trained longer (Chinchilla reallocation).</item>\\n<item>A4. Comparisons at fixed inference FLOPs per generated token, not fixed wall-clock.</item>\\n<item>A5. Primary alignment detectors at frontier scale: AlpacaEval-2 win-rate, IFEval, refusal-harm tradeoff. MMLU/GSM8K/HumanEval are capability sanity checks only.</item>\\n<item>A6. Headline outcome is a pre-registered statistical rule combining capability non-improvement and alignment-gap collapse at 70B.</item>\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"U1. Are partially-trained Llama-2 checkpoints publicly available, or must insufficient-pretraining be approximated from Chinchilla-style points or third-party reproductions?</item>\\n<item>U2. Is safety a positive component of the alignment DV (help-harm tradeoff curve) or a separate DV family?</item>\\n<item>U3. Comparisons at fixed inference FLOPs vs. at-recommended-precision (list price)?</item>\\n<item>U4. Include Llama-3 as reference point for positioning or restrict to Llama-2?</item>\\n<item>U5. Quantization: bf16-only or int4 acceptable?</item>\\n<item>U6. Human-eval in scope, or automatic benchmarks only?</item>\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-385df26f-7678-442f-8df0-76e10192318c", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
