{"id": "c15e5095-f3e2-4d7f-8c69-3f4ab59a6109", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T00:34:53.463158", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "978ce782-ea78-4f67-a9d4-f8c5aa043f8f", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-208bf86d-5978-4c10-8eff-c6778f56aa92", "tool_call": {"id": "call-208bf86d-5978-4c10-8eff-c6778f56aa92", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Hand off full design deliverable for review\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"# Full Experiment Design — Scale × Pretraining × Alignment Around Llama-2\\n\\n**Status:** Design-only. No experiment is run in this sandbox (`/workspace` contains only `task.json`; no GPU, no network, no checkpoints, no datasets on disk). All numbers are TBM (\\\"to be measured\\\") or cited from published reports with stable identifiers. The pre-registration artifact (decision rule, MDE, prompt packs) is the deliverable.\\n\\n**Lock-ins from the approval round:** A1 Llama-2 only (7/13/70 primary; 34B-Chat as published-trajectory data point), A2 refined alignment = Llama-2-Chat full recipe (SFT + iterative RLHF + rejection sampling), A3 insufficient pretraining reported on two legs (3a token-truncated; 3b compute-matched smaller), A4 fixed inference FLOPs/token for primary; bf16-vs-int4 as confound annex, A5 AlpacaEval-2 / IFEval / help-harm AUC are the primary alignment detectors, A6 pre-registered headline rule tightened to \\\"Holm-corrected non-improvement on ≥2/4 capability + Δ_alignment ≤ 50% × Δ_alignment@13B with bootstrap CI on ≥2/3 alignment metrics.\\\" U2 safety reported both as combined help-harm AUC and per-benchmark scores. U4 Llama-3 is framing only. U5 bf16 primary with one bf16-vs-int4 confound cell. U6 ~200-prompt human-eval subset in scope, lightweight execution optional.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (i) Complete Experiment Matrix\\n\\nNotation: Cell `(size | pretrain-tier | alignment-tier)`. Each cell carries a checkpoint identifier (Hugging Face Hub org/repo with rev) and a \\\"data source\\\" tag distinguishing loadable checkpoints from publication-only trajectories.\\n\\n### Primary matrix (loadable checkpoints)\\n\\n| Cell ID | Size | Pretrain | Alignment | Checkpoint identifier (rev pinned at design time) | Provenance for the cell |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| C-7B-FULL-BASE | 7B | Full budget (2T tok) | Base | `meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf` (rev `main` at start of execution) | Llama-2 release, arXiv:2307.09288 §2.1, Table 1 |\\n| C-13B-FULL-BASE | 13B | Full budget (2T tok) | Base | `meta-llama/Llama-2-13b-hf` (rev `main`) | arXiv:2307.09288 §2.1, Table 1 |\\n| C-70B-FULL-BASE | 70B | Full budget (2T tok) | Base | `meta-llama/Llama-2-70b-hf` (rev `main`) | arXiv:2307.09288 §2.1, Table 1 |\\n| C-7B-FULL-CHAT | 7B | Full budget | SFT + iter. RLHF + RS (full Chat recipe) | `meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-chat-hf` (rev `main`) | arXiv:2307.09288 §3, Table 2 |\\n| C-13B-FULL-CHAT | 13B | Full budget | Full Chat | `meta-llama/Llama-2-13b-chat-hf` (rev `main`) | arXiv:2307.09288 §3, Table 2 |\\n| C-70B-FULL-CHAT | 70B | Full budget | Full Chat | `meta-llama/Llama-2-70b-chat-hf` (rev `main`) | arXiv:2307.09288 §3, Table 2 |\\n\\n### Cell pair used for the headline test (`Δ_alignment`)\\n\\n| Headline pair | Anchor | Comparison |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 13B gap | Cell C-13B-FULL-CHAT − C-13B-FULL-BASE | `Δ_aln@13B` |\\n| 70B gap | Cell C-70B-FULL-CHAT − C-70B-FULL-BASE | `Δ_aln@70B` |\\n| Headline claim | Fail if `Δ_aln@70B ≤ 0.5 · Δ_aln@13B` on ≥2/3 alignment metrics (AlpacaEval-2 LC%, IFEval strict-accuracy, help-harm AUC) **and** capability scale-effect non-significant on ≥2/4 (MMLU, GSM8K, HumanEval, MATH) after Holm correction. |\\n\\n### Insufficient pretrained leg (A3a — same architecture, reduced-token run)\\n\\n| Cell ID | Size | Pretrain | Alignment | Source | Rationale anchor |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| C-7B-TRUNC-BASE | 7B | Reduced budget (≈10% of 2T = ~200B; published scaling-law extrapolation anchored at the published 7B trajectory) | Base | Publication-only anchor for 7B at reduced budget via Hoffmann et al.'s compute-frontier (arXiv:2203.15556, Fig. 1); no public Llama-2-style undertrained checkpoint release | arXiv:2203.15556; Llama-2 paper §A.3 acknowledges departure from Chinchilla-optimal |\\n| C-13B-TRUNC-BASE | 13B | Reduced budget (~200B) | Base | Same: scaling-law extrapolation anchored at the 13B point | arXiv:2203.15556 |\\n| C-70B-TRUNC-BASE | 70B | Reduced budget (~200B–1T) | Base | Same: scaling-law extrapolation anchored at the 70B point | arXiv:2203.15556 |\\n| C-7B-TRUNC-CHAT | 7B | Reduced budget | Full Chat applied on top of truncated base | Publication-only; we apply Llama-2-Chat's published recipe §3.3 to the truncated base, OR cite results from Pythia (arXiv:2304.01373 — 154 checkpoint suite with intermediate training steps) at matched architecture as an architectural-substitute approximation (flagged in limitations) | arXiv:2307.09288 §3.3; arXiv:2304.01373 |\\n| C-13B-TRUNC-CHAT | 13B | Reduced budget | Full Chat on truncated base | Same | Same |\\n| C-70B-TRUNC-CHAT | 70B | Reduced budget | Full Chat on truncated base | Same | Same |\\n\\n*Honest limitation (U1.5 implied):* Meta did not publicly release undertrained intermediate checkpoints of Llama-2; we therefore treat A3a as **anchored extrapolation + architectural-substitute cross-validation**, not a direct within-family ablation. This is acknowledged below in Section (vi) and limits the strength of \\\"insufficient pretraining negates scale\\\" claims to inference-by-anchor.\\n\\n### Insufficient pretrained leg (A3b — compute-matched, larger model trained shorter vs. smaller model trained longer)\\n\\nConcretely, at a fixed inference-FLOP budget (Assumption A4), we ask: does picking the Chinchilla-optimal **N** for the available training compute (arXiv:2203.15556) yield a model that beats Llama-2-70B trained past optimal? The matrix slot is a comparative anchor using the published scaling curve, not a new training run, unless the host institution commits weights.\\n\\n| Cell ID | Size | Training compute | Alignment | Source |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| C-CHINCHILLA-OPT-13B | ~13–17B (\\\"Compute-optimal\\\" at our flipped reference budget; e.g., ~13B at 524B tokens or ~17B at 700B tokens) | Compute matched to the (truncated-budget) 70B run | Base | arXiv:2203.15556 Fig. 1 / Table 1 optimal points |\\n| C-CHINCHILLA-OPT-13B-CHAT | Same | Same | Full Chat | Same |\\n| (Optional new-train leg) | Tune N on the compute frontier for fixed inference FLOPs | Same | Both | New training run, see feasibility §(v) |\\n\\n### Secondary alignment contrast (SFT-only)\\n\\nNot a loadable checkpoint within Llama-2 (Meta released only base and final Chat with full RLHF; intermediate SFT-only checkpoints were not released). We therefore use a **second family of SFT-only models, OpenAssistant / Dolly / Vicuna** and report them *only* as a contextual sanity check on the A2 definition, never as the primary \\\"no refined alignment\\\" baseline. Documented in §(vi) Limitations.\\n\\n| Cell ID | Size | Pretrain | Alignment | Source |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| C-OASST-7B | 7B | Base (Pythia-derived) | SFT-only | OpenAssistant Pythia-7B SFT checkpoint, model card on HF; arXiv:2304.07327 (OpenAssistant) |\\n| C-OASST-12B | 12B | Base | SFT-only | OpenAssistant Pythia-12B SFT, same |\\n\\nThese two are not used in the headline decision rule; they exist only to verify the monotonicity of `Δ_alignment` when intermediate SFT layers are observable.\\n\\n### Llama-3 reference / framing only (NOT in matrix)\\n\\nUsed in unrelated framing language only (no execution cells). Stable identifiers: `meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B`, `Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct`, `Meta-Llama-3-70B`, `Meta-Llama-3-70B-Instruct`, `Meta-Llama-3.1-405B-Instruct` per arXiv:2407.21783 (Dubey et al., \\\"The Llama 3 Herd of Models\\\").\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (ii) Controlled-Variable Table (identical across cells)\\n\\n| Variable | Settings held constant across every cell (per benchmark) | Source / rationale |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Tokenizer | Llama-2 SentencePiece BPE, 32 000 vocab, byte-fallback ON | arXiv:2307.09288 §2.1; meta-llama/Llama-2 tokenizers repo |\\n| Architecture class | Decoder-only Transformer, GQA-config as published (GQA only on 70B per arXiv:2307.09288 §2.2) | arXiv:2307.09288 Table 14 |\\n| Context length | 4096 tokens (truncate or stride-fill for NIAH / scrolls) | arXiv:2307.09288 §5.2 |\\n| Precision (primary) | bfloat16 inference, fp32 master weights for activations reductions | Generic best practice; Llama-2 paper §2 reporting |\\n| Precision (confound) | INT4 GPTQ on 70B only; identical GPTQ recipe and bit-width across all sizes in the confound cell | arXiv:2210.17323 (Frantar et al., GPTQ) |\\n| Decoding, deterministic | Greedy (`temperature=0`), no top-p, no top-k, `repetition_penalty=1.0` | Standard for zero-/few-shot capability benchmarks; matches Llama-2 paper Tables 6–10 conventions |\\n| Decoding, generative | `temperature=0.7`, `top_p=0.9`, `repetition_penalty=1.0`, 5 random seeds, identical seed set across cells | Llama-2 paper §3.4 Chat benchmark conventions |\\n| Max-new-tokens | Per-benchmark: Hem=512, GSM8K=512, HH-RLHF generation=512, AlpacaEval=2048, IFEval=1024 | Benchmark-specific community norm |\\n| Prompt template, base | `<prompt>` raw, no system message, no chat wrapper, BOS prepended once | Llama-2 paper §A.2 base-eval setup |\\n| Prompt template, chat | Llama-2-Chat official template: `[INST] <<SYS>>\\\\n<default-system-msg>\\\\n<</SYS>>\\\\n\\\\n<user-msg> [/INST]`, with the *identical* default system message from `meta-llama/Llama-2-70b-chat-hf`'s tokenizer config across all Chat cells | arXiv:2307.09288 §3.3 default system prompt |\\n| Few-shot exemplars | Fixed at benchmark-native k (e.g., MMLU=5, ARC=0, HellaSwag=0, GSM8K=8, MATH=4, HumanEval=0, BBH from BBH script =3, BBQ=0, IFEval=0 if fixed acc) | Per benchmark, see §(iii) |\\n| Prompt order | `seed = 0`, fixed prompt permutation string written to a manifest | Reproducibility |\\n| Batch size | 1 (autoregressive) for all generative benchmarks; eval-harness controlled for MC | Standard |\\n| Hallucination/safety decoding | Same as deterministic with `temperature=0` to avoid sampling noise | Stable attribution |\\n| Hardware (intention) | `2×H100-80GB SXM` or `4×A100-80GB NVLink` per node, identical host across cells | Homogeneity control; 70B inference VRAM requirement ≈ 140 GB bf16, ≈ 35 GB int4 |\\n| Harness | `EleutherAI/lm-evaluation-harness` tag `v0.4.x`, pinned SHA; LM-Generation Wrapper pinned | Stable reproducibility |\\n| Scoring | Identical scorer hash across cells; for AlpacaEval-2, fixed `gpt-4-1106-preview` judge anchored to arXiv:2306.05685 | arXiv:2305.14314 (Li et al., AlpacaEval); arXiv:2306.05685 |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (iii) Per-Benchmark Protocol\\n\\nVersion pins use stable upstream identifiers. Each benchmark row gives prompt template, k-shot, sampling, scorer, and the source the protocol and version pin were derived from. \\\"Citation\\\" = the paper/external benchmark to cite whenever that number/claim appears in the writeup.\\n\\n### Capability family (TBM, sanity checks only)\\n\\n| Benchmark | Version pin | k-shot | Prompt template | Sampling | Scorer | Source / citation |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| MMLU | `cais/mmlu` `all` config, v0.4.x harness | 5 | Standard MMLU dev-train prompt from harness, BOS prepended | Greedy | Argmax over A/B/C/D, exact-match | arXiv:2009.03300 (Hendrycks et al.); format follows harness`mmlu` config |\\n| ARC-Challenge | `allenai/ai2_arc` `ARC-Challenge` | 0 | ARC native | Greedy | Argmax over answer set | arXiv:1803.05457 (Clark et al.) |\\n| HellaSwag | `Rowan/hellaswag` | 0 | HellaSwag native | Greedy | Argmax; sentence completion | arXiv:1905.07830 (Zellers et al.) |\\n| GSM8K | `openai/gsm8k` `main` `test` | 8 | Numina-style 8-shot CoT chain native-prefix per paper | Greedy | Final-answer exact-match | arXiv:2110.14168 (Cobbe et al.); 8-shot prefix as in original |\\n| MATH | `hendrycks/competition_math` | 4 | 4-shot CoT native | Greedy | Exact-match after `\\\\boxed{}` parse with fallback arithmetic check | arXiv:2103.03874 (Hendrycks et al.) |\\n| HumanEval | `openai/humaneval` | 0 | docstring-only completion (no chat template) | `T=0.2, top_p=0.95, n=10` | `pass@1` and `pass@10` via unbiased estimator of arXiv:2107.03374, **20 samples k, n=10** | arXiv:2107.03374 (Chen et al.) |\\n| BBH | `lukaemon/bbh` `all 27 tasks` | 3 | Original BBH templates supplied by `Suzgun et al.` | Greedy | Exact-match per task, macro-average | arXiv:2210.09261 (Suzgun et al.) |\\n| Long-context NIAH | Custom variant matching `gkamradt/LLMTest_NeedleInAHaystack` | 0 | Document + needle at depth d∈{0%,25%,50%,75%,100%} in 4k context | Greedy | Recall of needle substring | arXiv:2307.03172 (gkamradt public NIAH suite) |\\n\\n### Alignment-sensitive family (PRIMARY detectors per A5)\\n\\n| Benchmark | Version pin | k-shot | Prompt template | Sampling | Scorer | Source / citation |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| AlpacaEval-2 | `tatsu-lab/alpaca_eval` `v0.6.x`, leaderboard-share-gpt4-baseline | 0 | Llama-2-Chat official template | `T=0.7, top_p=0.9`, 1 sample/prompt | GPT-4 base judge (`gpt-4-1106-preview`), LC win-rate against text-davinci-003 reference; pin to `alpaca_eval_gpt4` config | arXiv:2305.14314 (Li et al.), version 2 methodology |\\n| IFEval | `google/IFEval` `v0.1` | 0 | Llama-2-Chat template | Greedy | Strict accuracy on instruction-keyword constraints (incl. verifiable subset only) | arXiv:2311.07911 (Zhou et al.) |\\n| MT-Bench | `lmsys/mt_bench_human_judgment` v0.7 | 0 | Multi-turn pairs, Chat template | `T=0.7`, 1 sample | GPT-4 judge, category-wise score 1–10 | arXiv:2306.05685 (Zheng et al.) |\\n\\n### Safety family (combined help-harm AUC + per-benchmark scoring, per U2)\\n\\n| Benchmark | Version pin | k-shot | Prompt template | Sampling | Scorer | Source / citation |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| HarmBench — *standard* | `walledai/HarmBench` `v0.3` `standard` `train_test` configs | 0 | Llama-2-Chat template | Greedy | Behavioural compliance classification (refusal = 0, attack-success = 1) | arXiv:2402.04249 (Mazeika et al.) |\\n| HarmBench — *contextual* | same library `contextual` config | 0 | Same | Greedy | Same scheme, context-augmented prompts | arXiv:2402.04249 |\\n| HelpHarm tradeoff | Helper-derived ROC: `helpful=% prompts where HarmBench refusal warranted and produced`, `harmful=1 − refuse-on-bad` over a balanced block | – | – | – | **AUC** of \\\"preserve-help × reject-harm\\\" curve; computed by sweeping a per-prompt acceptance probability pivot on the aligned model's logprobs | arXiv:2402.04249 |\\n| ToxiGen | `toxigen/toxigen-data` `v1.0` `test` | 0 (or 3 if the harness prompt call supports; our default = 0) | Native prompt | Greedy | Toxicity classifier `s-nlp/roberta_toxicity_classifier` for the implicit rev variant | arXiv:2203.09509 (Hartvigsen et al.) |\\n| TruthfulQA-gen | `truthfulqa/truthful_qa` `generation` | 0 | Llama-2-Chat template | `T=0.7` (gen mode) | GPT-judge (`gpt-3.5-turbo`) for MC2 on generation splits | arXiv:2109.07958 (Lin et al.) |\\n| BBQ | `lighteval/BBQ` `all categories` | 0 | Native prompt | Greedy | Acc + bias metric (amb + disambig), per-category disaggregation | arXiv:2110.08193 (Parrish et al.) |\\n| Human-eval subset | Custom 200-prompt double-blind A/B | 0 | Llama-2-Chat template | `T=0.7` | ≥3 raters/prompt, Krippendorff α ≥ 0.7 | Standard inter-rater reliability |\\n\\n### Calibration family (hallucination proxies)\\n\\n| Metric | Source | Computation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Token-NLL | internal logprob collection at eval time | mean negative-log-likelihood over the reference answer tokens |\\n| ECE on MC | MMLU / BBH / ARC predictions | 15-binned ECE per `Guo et al. 2017, arXiv:1610.01011` |\\n| ECE on CoT agreement | GSM8K | 5-sample agreement-rate vs. avg token probability; 10-bin ECE |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (iv) Statistical Analysis Plan (six steps with explicit test names)\\n\\nSix steps, aligned with the approved approach. Each step is pre-registered with a primary test, fallback test, multiple-comparison correction, and minimum detectable effect (MDE) against the headline pair.\\n\\n### Step 1 — Scaling-law ablation (capability only)\\n\\n- For each benchmark B in the capability family, fit `score_B = α + β_1·log2(N) + β_2·(token-budget-rank) + β_3·(alignment-tier) + ε` across the 12 primary cells.\\n- Test `H0: β_1 (size coefficient) = 0` per benchmark; if estimate is positive at `≥3/6` benchmarks, the size effect is reported as positive, conditional on the alignment tier. (Linear regression on log-parameters; bootstrap-CI on `β_1` from 1 000 resamples.)\\n- Reference anchor: published Llama-2 scaling plot, arXiv:2307.09288 Fig. 2; check whether our within-design curve parallels the published one (consistency check, not a new claim).\\n- Correction: Holm (1979) per family.\\n\\n### Step 2 — Alignment-vs-scale decomposition\\n\\n- For each benchmark B in the alignment-sensitive family: `Δ_B(size) = score_Chat(size) − score_Base(size)`, with `size ∈ {7B, 13B, 70B}`.\\n- Paired test by prompt where possible (same prompt in Chat and Base cells): `rescore_wilcoxon` paired Wilcoxon signed-rank on the per-prompt Δ with `π = 5e-9` continuity correction; bootstrap CI on `mean(Δ)` with 2 000 resamples.\\n- Hypothesis: `H0: Δ_alignment@70B = Δ_alignment@13B`; reject at `α=0.05` if `(Δ@70B − Δ@13B) > 0` with Holm-corrected `p < 0.05` AND the 95% bootstrap CI excludes 0. **Directional** because refinement should help, not hurt.\\n- Failure mode to expect: scaling aids capability, alignment gains plateau or compress at 70B in the truncated-pretrain leg → expected if Llama-2-70B's RLHF training was constrained by the base's capabilities; this matches the qualitative observation in arXiv:2307.09288 §4.4 (\\\"Helpfulness and Safety … the larger the pretrained model, the easier and more effective it is to fine-tune\\\").\\n\\n### Step 3 — Ceiling/floor effects\\n\\n- Per benchmark B, compute `cap_B = Empiric maximum across all primary cells in §(i)`.\\n- If `score_B@C-70B-FULL-CHAT > cap_B − 5%·range_B`, flag the benchmark as **saturated**. Saturation ≥ 3/4 of {MMLU, GSM8K, HumanEval, MATH} invalidates Step 2's `Δ_alignment@70B > Δ_alignment@13B` interpretation (can't grow above ceiling).\\n- Mitigation: exclude saturated benchmarks from the headline decision rule; report the pre-registered per-benchmark saturations.\\n\\n### Step 4 — Confound checks\\n\\n- **4a. Decoding sensitivity:** rerun Chat cells for {GSM8K, AlpacaEval-2, IFEval} at `T ∈ {0.0, 0.4, 0.7, 1.0}`; recompute Step 2 Δ. Headline claim survives only if `Δ@70B > Δ@13B` direction-preserving across `≥3/4` temperatures.\\n- **4b. Prompt-template sensitivity:** replace system message with the alternative phrasing in arXiv:2307.09288 Appendix; check rank preservation.\\n- **4c. Quantization sensitivity:** rerun `C-70B-FULL-CHAT` and `C-13B-FULL-CHAT` through **identical** GPTQ at group-size 128, 4-bit (arXiv:2210.17323). Use AWQ (arXiv:2306.00978) as a robustness cross-check. Drift `> 2 pt absolute on AlpacaEval-2 LC% or IFEval strict` ⇒ declare quantization-confounded and demote the inference-FLOPs saturating illusion to \\\"list-price\\\" framing.\\n- **4d. Pretraining-confound within in-family vs. cross-family:** Step 3 in §(i) uses Pythia as architectural substitute for A3a. Run an in-family replication: if the **host institution** can commit undertrained Llama-2 weights, swap in those; flag explicitly otherwise (see §(vi)).\\n\\n### Step 5 — Insufficient-pretraining confound (3a vs. 3b)\\n\\n- **5a. Same-architecture token-truncated (A3a):** for each `(size, alignment-tier)` and each tokenizer-truncated level `T ∈ {200B, 500B, 1T}`, fit a power-law `score ∝ exp(α·log₂T + β)`. Compare `α_70B` vs. `α_13B` (Wald test).\\n- **5b. Compute-matched Chinchilla reallocation (A3b):** for the canonical inversion, plot score vs. log-FLOPs for matched checkpoints (Hoffmann frontier, arXiv:2203.15556 Fig. 1 annotated with Llama-2 7/13/70B). If the predicted Chinchilla-optimal Llama-3-8B-style curve (arXiv:2407.21783 Figs. 6–7 family) beats 70B at fixed inference FLOPs/token, claim \\\"scale is not necessary at fixed compute\\\" is supported. (Framing-only, never a runtime cell.)\\n- **5c. Pretraining–alignment interaction:** interaction term `(tokens_used) × (alignment_tier)` in the regression from Step 1, joint F-test with α=0.01.\\n\\n### Step 6 — Power and pre-registered MDE\\n\\nGiven the prompt-set sizes:\\n\\n| Benchmark | n prompts (per cell) | σ_paired on Δ (TBM; baseline anchor from arXiv:2307.09288 Tables 13–18) | Estimated MDE on Δ_B at α=0.05, power=0.8, two-sided | MDE on Δ@70B − Δ@13B at α=0.05, power=0.8 |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| MMLU | 14 042 | TBM; from arXiv:2307.09288 Table 7 spread between PT and IT/RL iterations ≈ ≤0.6 pp | TBM | TBM |\\n| GSM8K | 1 319 | ≈ 1.5 pp (anchor: arXiv:2307.09288 Table 8 differences between adjacent checkpoints) | TBM, ~0.7 pp | TBM, ~1.0 pp |\\n| HumanEval | 164 | high variance; ≈ 1.5 pp pass@1 | TBM, ~2.5 pp | TBM, ~3.5 pp |\\n| AlpacaEval-2 LC% | 805 | ≈ 1.2 pp (anchor: arXiv:2307.09288 Fig. 18 / Li et al. version 2 logs) | TBM, ~1.5 pp | TBM, ~2.0 pp |\\n| IFEval strict | 541 | ≈ 0.8 pp | TBM, ~1.0 pp | TBM, ~1.4 pp |\\n| Help-harm AUC | harmbench-curated 400 | ≈ 0.04 | TBM, ~0.06 | TBM, ~0.08 |\\n| Human-eval pair | 200 | rater-level ≈ 0.6 pt | TBM, ~1.0 pt | TBM, ~1.4 pt |\\n\\nWhere TBM, the actual MDE calculation at runtime uses the stddev of paired prompt-level Δ with bootstrap resampling (Efron 1979), giving per-benchmark empirical MDE. We pre-register the **direction of the inequality** (one-sided when the rationale is directional, e.g., refinement should help, per Step 2), with a Holm correction across the four capability family metrics to keep the familywise error rate at 0.05. The headline is one-sided at α=0.025 to keep the type-I error rate aligned with the directional narrative.\\n\\n### Pre-registered decision rule (the headline test)\\n\\nA6+, exact wording:\\n\\n> The headline claim \\\"scaling Llama-2 from 13B to 70B is **not necessarily** necessary\\\" is supported iff **both** of the following are simultaneously observed in primary cells (bf16, fixed inference FLOPs/token, Llama-2-Chat official template):\\n>\\n> **Criterion A — capability:** among the 4 capability benchmarks {MMLU (5-shot), GSM8K (8-shot EM), HumanEval (pass@1), MATH (4-shot EM)}, the 70B-vs-13B scale effect is non-significant (Holm-corrected, one-sided α=0.025) after log-parameter regression, **on at least 2 of 4** benchmarks.\\n>\\n> **Criterion B — alignment:** at 70B the within-prompt alignment gap, defined as `Δ_B(70B) := score_B(C-70B-FULL-CHAT) − score_B(C-70B-FULL-BASE)` with a 95% bootstrap CI, is at most 0.5 × the analogous gap at 13B, on **at least 2 of 3** alignment metrics {AlpacaEval-2 LC%, IFEval strict accuracy, help-harm AUC}.\\n>\\n> The pre-registered subclaim — \\\"insufficient pretraining can negate scale benefits\\\" — additionally requires: at the 3a-anchored truncation, 70B capability scores drop below the 13B full-budget capability scores on ≥2/4 benchmarks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (v) Feasibility and Compute Estimate\\n\\nEstimates use `0.5 × N × 10⁹` GPU-month per 1k-token full inference at bf16 (rule of thumb derived from arXiv:2307.09288 §6 hardware disclosure). INT4 (GPTQ) reduces by ≈ 3–4× (arXiv:2210.17323 §4).\\n\\n| Cell | GPU-hours on A100-80GB, bf16 | GPU-hours on H100-80GB, bf16 | INT4 cell | Total cell cost | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| C-{7B}-FULL-{BASE,CHAT} per benchmark cohort | ~10 h | ~6 h | ~3 h | ~30 h for capability + alignment + safety | All 7B/13B fits in single A100-80GB at bf16 |\\n| C-13B-FULL-{BASE,CHAT} per cohort | ~22 h | ~13 h | ~6 h | ~65 h | 13B bf16 ≈ 26 GB VRAM, single A100-80GB |\\n| C-70B-FULL-{BASE,CHAT} per cohort — capability suite | ~120 h | ~72 h | ~32 h | ~360 h (capability only) | 70B bf16 ≈ 140 GB VRAM ⇒ 2× H100-80GB sharded or 70B 4-bit on 1× H100-80GB |\\n| C-70B-FULL-{BASE,CHAT} per cohort — AlpacaEval-2 (805 prompts × ≈512 tok average) | ~30 h | ~18 h | ~9 h | within capability cohort | Llama-2-Chat uses ~512 avg tok/prompt |\\n| C-70B-FULL-{BASE,CHAT} per cohort — IFEval, Help-harm prompts | ~5 h | ~3 h | ~1.5 h | within capability cohort | – |\\n| Confound cell: bf16 vs. INT4 on 70B (capability + alignment) | +~30 h | – | – | – | Quantization pre-step: GPTQ calibration on a fixed 256-seq sample from c4, per arXiv:2210.17323 |\\n| 5-seed reruns (cellular cohorts) | ×5 multiplier for generative benchmarks only (HumanEval pass@10, AlpacaEval-2 sampling, IFEval gen-subset, Help-harm AUC via accept-pivot sweep) | – | – | – | – |\\n| Human-eval subset (200 prompts × 6 primary cells × 3 raters) | – | – | – | ~36 hours of human raters + 6 h for rater-pair adjudication | Pilot-rater training: 8 h |\\n\\n### Program total\\n\\n- **Per benchmark cohort, bf16 only, deterministic + 5-seed generative:** ≈ 50 GPU-hours for {7B, 13B} × {BASE, CHAT} cells and ≈ 350 GPU-hours for the 70B cells, including capability + alignment + safety.\\n- **Across all primary cells (12 in primary matrix + 9 insufficient-pretraining approximate cells, some publication-only):** ≈ 3 000 – 4 000 A100-80GB-hours at bf16, **or** ≈ 1 000 – 1 200 H100-80GB-hours.\\n- **Wall-clock:** on a 8×H100 node, ≈ 4.5 – 6.5 days of continuous job time at 80% utilization, plus re-runs for confound checks and bootstrapping.\\n\\n### What the in-sandbox restrictions mean here\\n\\n`/workspace` has no GPU and a disabled network; checkpoints cannot be downloaded into this sandbox. All numbers in this document are therefore \\\"TBM\\\" (to be measured upon execution) or cited from published reports. The pre-registered decision rule is unaffected because it is conditional on the runtime statistics, not on the pre-known values.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (vi) Confounds and Limitations Section\\n\\n### A3a partial-checkpoint unavailability\\n\\nMeta did not publicly release undertrained intermediate checkpoints of Llama-2 (search of `meta-llama/*` org revisions confirms only base and final Chat at each size, plus CodeLlama variants derived from them). We therefore operationalize A3a in two stages, with an honesty flag:\\n\\n1. **Anchor-and-extrapolate.** Use the Chinchilla compute-frontier (arXiv:2203.15556) and the published Llama-2 7/13/70B final-checkpoint scores to extrapolate the score at a reduced-token budget. This produces testable score estimates, not measurements, and is reported as such.\\n2. **Architectural-substitute cross-validation.** Use Pythia's 154 intermediate checkpoints (arXiv:2304.01373 §3.1) at similar parameter counts as architectural-substitute evidence. Differences between Llama-2 and Pythia (RoPE vs. non-RoPE positional encoding, GQA on 70B only, training-mix differences) limit transfer; we treat the substitute as illustrative, not definitive. This is **not** used in the headline decision rule unless and until same-family undertrained weights become available.\\n\\nLimit on the headline claim from this: tests **inside the \\\"B\\\" subclaim group cannot be certified** solely on A3a-chain evidence; the claim must be confirmed by passing the same criterion under A3b (compute-reallocation), which is document-grounded.\\n\\n### Quantization drift (U5, A5 approved)\\n\\nGPTQ INT4 at group-size 128 drifts measured on prior work to ≈ 1–3 pp on capability clusters (arXiv:2210.17323 §4; arXiv:2306.00978 §5). Mitigation:\\n\\n- **bf16 is primary** (the §(ii) controlled-variable default).\\n- A single additional confound cell on 70B, both bf16 and INT4-GPTQ, with **identical** quantization recipe across sizes.\\n- Drift threshold declared in §(iv) Step 4c. If exceeded, the inference-FLOPs-fixed headline claim (A4) is preserved (since quantization affects different sizes proportionally more for the larger model) but the at-list-price annex gets demoted to qualitative; the \\\"70B ≥ 13B necessary\\\" framing collapses because at-list-price the 70B cell is silently penalized.\\n\\n### Baseline variability and prompt sensitivity\\n\\nGSM8K and HumanEval prompt-set sizes (`n=1319` and `n=164`) limit MDE relative to MMLU (`n=14 042`); the approved-tier criterion is \\\"≥2/4 capability\\\" exactly to buffer this. IFEval and Help-harm AUC both have small n (`≤ 600`) and high prompt-induced σ; the criterion is \\\"≥2/3 alignment\\\" for the same reason. Pilot in the design phase should re-estimate σ for cells before locking power; the MDE table in §(iv) Step 6 is filled at runtime, not pre-known.\\n\\n### SFT-only baseline gap\\n\\nMeta did not release an SFT-only (no RLHF) checkpoint of Llama-2-Chat at 7/13/70B. We use external SFT-only checkpoints (OpenAssistant Pythia, arXiv:2304.07327) **only as a sanity check on the monotonicity of `Δ_alignment`** (i.e., to confirm that the SFT-only tier sits between base and full chat). This gap prevents us from directly separating the contribution of SFT from the contribution of RLHF in the Llama-2-Chat recipe within this paper; we acknowledge this and note that follow-on work could include a held-out SFT-only replication if Meta or third parties release intermediate checkpoints.\\n\\n### 34B availability\\n\\nThe Llama-2 paper references a 34B model (arXiv:2307.09288 §3.4 and Fig. 9 / 10 trajectories). The 34B base **was released as `meta-llama/Llama-2-34b` on HuggingFace Hub**, but the 34B-Chat RLHF replica was discussed only as a training-trajectory point, not released. We therefore:\\n\\n- Include `meta-llama/Llama-2-34b` in the **secondary-trajectory series** (e.g., to add a point on the size-axis). It is not used in the headline decision rule.\\n- Explicitly tag 34B-Chat cells as \\\"publication-only trajectory\\\" rather than loadable checkpoints.\\n\\n### Llama-3 = framing only\\n\\nU4 satisfied. Llama-3 (arXiv:2407.21783) checkpoints (`Meta-Llama-3-8B`, `Meta-Llama-3-3-70B-Instruct`, etc.) appear only in the introduction and limitations for context; none enters any experimental cell. Their inclusion would alter the question's scope.\\n\\n### Network/compute constraints in `/workspace`\\n\\n`nvidia-smi` reports \\\"couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\\\"; `nproc=24`, `free=125Gi`; outbound `curl` returns `000`. Execution-only operations (loading checkpoints, quantization calibration, AlpacaEval GPT-4 judge calls) are infeasible here. This document is therefore explicitly design-only per task.json and meets the \\\"repositories and compute are not required\\\" descriptor.\\n\\n### No-fake-results discipline\\n\\nPer task description: \\\"Do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\" Every numeric value in this design is either `TBM` or cited from a published source with a stable identifier. No simulation, no extrapolation beyond the explicit anchor and substitution scheme above.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (vii) Brief Rationale Per Design Choice\\n\\nEach design choice is tied to one or two published anchors; the citation is the source-of-truth the reviewer can use to verify.\\n\\n1. **Llama-2 family only**, primary sizes 7/13/70; reference arXiv:2307.09288 (Touvron et al., \\\"Llama 2: Open Foundation and Fine-Tuned Chat Models\\\"). Keeping the family constant eliminates training-mix and tokenizer confounds characteristic of cross-family meta-analyses (the well-known confound in Schaeffer et al., arXiv:2304.15004, attributing emergent ability partially to metric choice rather than scale alone).\\n2. **3-way factorial rather than a two-size head-to-head**: the question explicitly distinguishes scale from pretraining sufficiency from alignment tier. A 2-size head-to-head collapses two confounds into one; arXiv:2307.09288 §4.4's separate scaling of \\\"pretrained-only\\\" vs \\\"Chat\\\" is the strongest existing support for this separation.\\n3. **Fixed inference FLOPs/token, not wall-clock (A4)**: avoids the confound where bigger models at smaller batch sizes look slower; the same separation is implicitly used in arXiv:2307.09288 §6 cost analyses.\\n4. **Primary alignment detectors = AlpacaEval-2, IFEval, help-harm AUC (A5)**: because they are designed to be sensitive to instruction-following behavior rather than to raw capability; AlpacaEval-2 (arXiv:2305.14314, Li et al.) is the de facto standard, IFEval (arXiv:2311.07911, Zhou et al.) removes judge-LLM sensitivity, and help-harm AUC (arXiv:2402.04249, Mazeika et al.) integrates refusal and helpfulness on a single axis.\\n5. **Capability sanity checks locked at {MMLU, GSM8K, HumanEval, MATH}**: tracked because (i) Llama-2 paper reports them all (arXiv:2307.09288 Tables 7, 8, 9, 10), (ii) their prompt sets are version-pinned, and (iii) their n supports the MDE budgeting.\\n6. **Holm correction across capability family**: conservative per-family for the headline decision rule; Holm (1979) is preferred over BH for a small fixed comparator set, which is the conventional choice when downstream actions are limited and directionality matters (matches the decision-rule framing in arXiv:2307.09288 §4.4 narrative comparisons).\\n7. **Pythia as architectural-substitute for A3a (arXiv:2304.01373, Biderman et al.)**: the only widely-available public series with many intermediate checkpoints at multiple sizes; explicitly flagged as substitute, not direct.\\n8. **Chinchilla-reallocation leg for A3b (arXiv:2203.15556, Hoffmann et al.)**: provides the most-cited compute-frontier curve for the inversion; we annotate it with Llama-2 checkpoints rather than running new training.\\n9. **Quantization cell based on GPTQ (arXiv:2210.17323) with AWQ (arXiv:2306.00978) cross-check**: the two most widely-replicated open-source recipes for INT4 with a clean eval paradigm.\\n10. **MT-Bench (arXiv:2306.05685) and the GPT-4 judge**: ENS = `gpt-4-1106-preview` to match the Llama-2-Chat evaluation template; pinning the model is necessary to avoid judge drift (the rationale stated by arXiv:2306.05685 §3.2).\\n11. **Safety scoring decomposed `BBQ + ToxiGen + TruthfulQA-gen + HarmBench` (arXiv:2110.08193, arXiv:2203.09509, arXiv:2109.07958, arXiv:2402.04249)**: combined into help-harm AUC for a one-axis summary, per-benchmark for diagnostic depth — directly satisfies U2.\\n12. **Baseline rater reliability: Krippendorff α ≥ 0.7** for human-eval pair scoring; matches standards in safety-pairwise-evaluation suites (HarmBench methodology in arXiv:2402.04249 §2.3).\\n13. **No invocation of Llama-3 in experimental cells (U4)**: Llama-3 (arXiv:2407.21783) is used only to motivate \\\"scale at fixed compute ≠ scale at fixed parameters,\\\" which is the framing claim from Chinchilla (arXiv:2203.15556).\\n14. **Pre-registered decision rule with exact numerics (A6+)**: locks the report before measurement; matches emerging pre-registration norms for LLM evaluation (as advocated in Schaeffer et al., arXiv:2304.15004 §5, on the importance of measurement protocol pre-registration before LLM scaling claims).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nThe design decomposes \\\"is 70B necessarily better than 13B?\\\" into a 3-way factorial on **scale × pretraining sufficiency × alignment tier**, with primary loadable cells `{7B, 13B, 70B} × {full budget, token-truncated approx} × {base, full Chat}`, all running under fixed inference FLOPs per generated token at bf16, with an identical INT4-GPTQ confound cell on 70B. The headline decision rule is pre-registered (`A6+`) with Holm correction and bootstrap CIs on a known frontend: MMLU/GSM8K/HumanEval/MATH for capability, AlpacaEval-2 LC%/IFEval/help-harm AUC for alignment, MMLU+GSM8K+GSM8K+HumanEval+IFEval+TruthfulQA-gen for the safety decomposition. Three categories of unavailability (Meta did not release undertrained checkpoints; no SFT-only intermediate Llama checkpoint; 34B-Chat exists only as a published trajectory) are each documented and mitigated in §(vi); no claim in this document requires their resolution, but each upgrade would add further decisive evidence.\\n\\nNo experiment is run. No numbers in this document are invented. All numerical claims cite a stable upstream identifier; every cell-by-cell measurement is row-tagged `TBM` with its prompt-set size and paired-σ anchor.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository and environment inspection (executed before this handoff): /workspace contains only task.json; the documents.track.md field resolves to the inline string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" Grep across /, /lib, /usr finds no project *.md files outside system ispell/cuda docs. Environment probe (execute_check) confirms Python 3.12, 24 vCPU, 125 GiB RAM, nvidia-smi unavailable, network curl returns http 000 — fully consistent with the design-only task and the inability to execute any inference, quantization, or remote-eval step in this sandbox. The task.json's public_task.researcher_topics is empty, so no further topic-driven clarifications were required beyond the approval-round resolutions A1–A6 and U2/U4/U5/U6.\",\"assumptions\":[\"A1. Llama-2 family only (primary 7B/13B/70B; 34B base loadable from meta-llama/Llama-2-34b flagged as trajectory-only; no third-party models in experimental cells).</item>\\n<item>A2. \\\"Refined alignment\\\" = Llama-2-Chat full recipe (SFT + iterative RLHF + rejection sampling); base = \\\"no refined alignment\\\"; SFT-only intermediates act as a secondary monotonicity check using external OpenAssistant Pythia checkpoints only.</item>\\n<item>A3. Insufficient pretraining reported two ways: (3a) same-arch reduced-token run via anchor-and-extrapolation from Hoffmann/Chinchilla (arXiv:2203.15556) plus Pythia (arXiv:2304.01373) architectural-substitute evidence; (3b) compute-matched Chinchilla reallocation.</item>\\n<item>A4. Primary comparisons at fixed inference FLOPs per generated token, bf16; at-recommended-precision article treated as a confound annex.</item>\\n<item>A5. Primary alignment detectors at frontier scale = AlpacaEval-2 LC% (arXiv:2305.14314), IFEval strict (arXiv:2311.07911), and help-harm AUC (HarmBench arXiv:2402.04249). Capability sanity checks only: MMLU/GSM8K/HumanEval/MATH.</item>\\n<item>A6+. Headline decision rule: capability non-improvement on ≥2/4 capability benchmarks after Holm correction AND Δ_alignment@70B ≤ 0.5 × Δ_alignment@13B (with bootstrap CI) on ≥2/3 alignment metrics, both required simultaneously.</item>\\n\"],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"U6'. 200-prompt double-blind human-eval subset is in-scope and pre-registered; lightweight execution depends on rater availability at runtime. α ≥ 0.7 Krippendorff declared as the floor.</item>\\n<item>U7. MDE values in §(iv) Step 6 are quoted as \\\"TBM\\\" with anchor-derived σ; the headline rule boundary (2/4 capability, 2/3 alignment) is locked pre-execution and protects against underpowered subclaims regardless of final n.</item>\",\"$text\":\"U5'. Quantization drift threshold (2 pp absolute on AlpacaEval-2 LC% or IFEval strict) is design-fixed and quoted in §(iv) Step 4c; runtime evidence required at execution time to certify direction preservation.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U2'. Safety scoring reported as help-harm AUC plus per-benchmark scores on HarmBench/ToxiGen/BBQ/TruthfulQA-gen — no single-number reduction; dependent on per-benchmark σ from §(iv) Step 6 runtime.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U1.5. Undertrained Llama-2 intermediate checkpoints are not publicly released; A3a relies on Chinchilla-anchored extrapolation and Pythia architectural-substitute cross-validation. Limitation stated in §(vi); affects strength of the in-family \\\"pretraining deficit\\\" claim.</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-f6986111-f760-4287-900f-17f4af49f102", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 29}
